 (L) "The learned Additional Attorney General while making his submission in favour of the leave petitioner found it difficult to support the leave petitioner, since confronted with the gazette notifications dated 24.03.1952 showing acquisition of rent receiving interest under Section 3(1) of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act in respect of the lands covered by Monza Raza Bazar under J.L. No. 2 and the gazette notification dated 29.02.1956 showing acquisition and vesting of the lands, in respect of which the rent receiving interest have been acquired under Section 43(2), 46 C an 46E(3) of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act,1950 in favour of the Government." 

 We are confronted with unnecessary and uncalled for litigations like the present one in spite of setting the self same issue connecting with "projabili property" by the Apex Court in its different decisions reported in 33 DLR (S.C.) 13 and 53, 27 DLR (S.C.) 109 and lastly in the case reported in 14 MLR (A.D.) 401. 

 9. Every court has inherent power for securing the ends of justice in any suit/proceeding; so, if the court from the admitted materials before it found that the pending litigation would ultimately brings no result in favour of the plaintiffs-petitioners can dismiss the suit/case before it; in that situation the court by exercising its inherent jurisdiction can dismiss or record the abatement of the suit or appeal; the inherent power has been approved by the precedents to the case of Abdul Jalil and others —vs- Islamic Bank Bangladesh Ltd. And others reported in 53 DLR(AD)-12, to the case of Sanatannessa Bewa —vs- Haipatullah Sarker & ors reported in 4] DLR(AD)-105; 

 Before enactment of State Acquisition and Tenancy Act,1950 there was Zamindary system in the Land Administration and Zamindar had two types of property, one projabli property i.e. land settled to the projas subject to payment of rent to the Zamindar and another, the khas property i.e. the property remained in possession of the Zamindar like his own house, garden, ponds etc.; in the projabili property though the Zamindar had only rent receiving interest but in the name of realization of that interest/rent from the projas, the servants under the Zamindar (Paik Peyada) usually oppressed the projas/raiyats which shocked the then political leaders and social reformer for the abolishing the Zamindari System; those political leaders and social reformer promised to the general public that if the country is liberated from British Rule they would abolish the Zamindari system and the projas under the Zamindar won Id be upgraded as Malik (owner) from the status of Projas; 

 “If the rent-receiving interests of a ward in ally estate under the charge of the Court are acquired under the provisions of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950, then, notwithstanding anything contained elsewhere in this Act, the remaining properties of such ward including khas lands under the charge and shall be managed in accordance with the provisions of this Act, as if such ward were a disqualified proprietor of an estate." 

 The Government as per said modes and procedure firstly published a gazette notification under Section 3 of the said SAT Act, 1950 Monday, March 24 in the year 1952 describing therein the description of the projabili property in the column No.2 and the name and address of the Zamindar/rent Receiver in the column No.1; 

 Before publication of the said projabili property gazette the Government had issued a notice under Section 3(A) and 4 of the said SAT Acti 950 asking the Zamindar/rent Receiver to submit a list of projabili property remained under possession of their projas and khas property under their ownership and possession with a penal clause in case of failure to submit the list as asked for; 

 "(a) Assessed the compensation against acquiring the Zamindari/rent reciving interest of the Zamindar under Section 33 of the SAT Act,1950, which was commonly known as Compensation Assessment Roll (CA Roll); 

 (b)Thereafter paid the said assessed compensation as per CA Roll to the Zamindar; 

 ©After payment of compensation to the Zamindar the then Government published a compensation payment gazette under Section 46E of the SAT Act, 1950 in the year 1956 declaring that the compensation assessed against acquisition of rent receiving interest of the Zamindar in the projabili property cited in the projabili property gazette published under Section 3 of the SAT Act, 1950 had been paid; 

 (d)After completing the aforesaid process for acquiring Zamindari/rent receiving interest, the property cited in the projabili property gazette were recorded in the names of concerned projas upgrading them as owners in the khatian as State Acquisition (S.A.) khatian after determining the land revenue under Section 24(1) of the SAT Act, 1950; as such, almost in all the S.A. Khatian 24(1) of the SAT Act, 1950 was cited as the basis of determining land development tax payable by the new owners by operation of law to the new rent receiver, the Government." 

 17. In spite of the aforesaid amended Section 8(A) of the Court of Wards Act, 1879 and P.O. No. 90 of 1972, when the previous Zamindars were found in oppression to the previous projas, some of the previous projas who became owners by operation of law, went to the Supreme Court for redress wherein apex Court of the Country gave observations and directions to the previous Zmindars/rent receivers not to claiming any right, title and interest in their previous projabili properties. 

 "It is now well settled on the authority of Jalil Ahmed vs. The province of East Pakistan decided by a Special Bench and affirmed by the Supreme Court of Pakistan in Tanvir Ahmed Siddiky vs. The province of East Pakistan that on the publication of the notification under section 3, of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, all rent-receiving interests including that of debater and waqf estate stood acquired and all non-retainable khas land also stood vested in the Government." 

 In 1963 notifications u/s 43 of the East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act have been published acquiring of all interests other than those already acquired under section 3 of the Act, completing all acquisition proceedings under the Act, All the notifications have been published in the official Gazette covering all districts of Bangladesh. Another fact is that the Bangladesh (Legal proceedings) (Third) Order, 1972 (P.O. 90 of 1972) was promulgated on 3rd August, 1972. This enactment has been promulgated long after all the acquisition proceedings were completed and long after the observations of the High Court made regarding the non-preparation of the compensation assessment-roll. President's Order No. 90 of 1972 clearly states that notwithstanding anything contained in any other law for the time being in force, on the commencement of this Order, all suits, appeals, applications, etc. pending in any court against the Government of Bangladesh in which the legality or validity of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, or of the East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy (Amendment) Ordinance, 1956 (E.B . Ordinance No. III of 1956) or of any other law making any amendment in the said Act, or of the acquisition of any property make there under is challenged in question shall abate, and all orders, including orders of injunction, etc. passed in such suits, appeals, etc. shall cease to have any effect and no court shall entertain and no person shall bring any fresh suit, appeal, application or other legal proceedings in which the legality or validity of any such law or acquisition is challenged or called in question. 

 "With the publication of notification under section 3 all rent-receiving interests including wakf and debuttor property stood acquired by and vested in the Government   after the notification issued under section 3 of the Act the status of the under raiyat became upgraded to that of raiyat. The rent receiving interests can be acquired even before the preparation of record of rights and publication of compensation assessment rolls'..... with the acquisition of the rent-receiving interests by the government all intermediary interests disappeared and a person holding the land under the government becomes a "tenant", becomes a "raiyat" or a "malik" of the land recognized by the government. 

 Consequently, the under-raiyat who actually holds the land becomes a tenant under the government. Superior landlord (i.e. the raiyat) of an under-raiyat by virtue of elimination of his rent-receiving interests disappears and the under-raiyat is upgraded to the status of a "raiyat"..... record of rights prepared and finally published under section 144 cannot be varied by any authority and remedy of wrong entry if any lies with the appropriate court' the property enlisted/shown in the "projabili property" Gazette are the properties of "projas" who have been accepted as "owners" and the "rent-receiving" interest whatsoever it had therein, of the jamindars all vested to the Government by operation of SAT Act, 1950."
